// ============================================================================
// EXPRESSION RESOLUTION · CALL SITE  (decomposition, and the conserved universe)
//
// In Java this layer DERIVES call sites from METHOD_INVOCATION nodes. In Python
// py_call_site is a base relation, so this file does the opposite job: it takes the
// given site and pulls out the pieces callee resolution needs, and it fixes the
// UNIVERSE over which conservation is asserted.
//
// THE UNIVERSE IS py_call_site, ONE ROW PER SITE. Every row must appear in
// call_chain_edge — resolved, or flagged. That is the invariant test/python's coverage
// guard checks, and it is the one failure mode a golden-file diff cannot see: you
// cannot notice the absence of something never recorded.
//
// THE ARGUMENT-ROLE TRAP, straight from the schema. edgeRole ARGUMENT is NOT unique to
// calls — a list/tuple/set display's children would wear it too if ELEMENT/KEY/VALUE
// did not exist, and a rule that joins edgeRole="ARGUMENT" to a call site WITHOUT also
// testing the parent's kind picked up 17% non-arguments (44 of 260) on the flow corpus.
// Every argument rule below is anchored on expr_node(_, "CALL", ...).
// ============================================================================

// ============================================================================
// THE SITE KEY IS THE CALL EXPRESSION HASH, NOT THE py_call_site HASH.
//
// py_call_site is 1:1 with its CALL expression (its PK chains off
// pyExpressionLinkHash), so either would identify a site. The expression hash is chosen
// because it is the ONLY key common to BOTH conserved inventories: py_call_site carries
// it in pyExpressionLinkHash and py_decorator carries it in its own
// pyExpressionLinkHash — and for a decorator written WITH parentheses
// (`@functools.wraps(fn)`, `@route("go")`) the two are THE SAME HASH.
//
// MEASURED CONSEQUENCE. Keyed on pyCallSiteUniqueHash instead, case 07-decorators
// dropped 4 sites — three @functools.wraps and one @route. The engine had resolved
// every one of them; the rows were simply filed under a key that neither the decorator
// inventory nor the coverage guard looks for. One key serves both inventories, and a
// parenthesised decorator then produces exactly ONE site instead of two.
// ============================================================================

// ── csite(SiteKey, PyCallSiteHash) — the bridge back to the base row ─────────
csite(e, s) :- call_decl("client", _, _, _, e, s).

// ── invocation_site(SiteKey, CallKind) ───────────────────────────────────────
// The conserved universe, tagged with the schema's own callKind so a report can filter
// by call shape without a second join. Client-only: a library call site is never
// staged (LIB_BODY), so there is nothing to enumerate on that side.
invocation_site(e, ck) :- call_decl("client", ck, _, _, e, _).

// ── call_recv_kind(SiteKey, ReceiverKind) ────────────────────────────────────
// The receiver SHAPE, rekeyed onto the site key. Every dispatch rule switches on this.
call_recv_kind(e, rk) :- csite(e, s), call_receiver("client", rk, _, _, s).

// ── call_expr(SiteHash, CallExprHash) / call_of_expr(CallExprHash, SiteHash) ──
// The 1:1 bridge between a site and its CALL expression node. Both directions, because
// half the joins start from the AST and half from the site.
// Identities now that the site key IS the expression hash — kept so every downstream
// rule reads unchanged, and so a future re-keying is a one-line change here.
call_expr(e, e) :- csite(e, _).
call_of_expr(e, e) :- csite(e, _).

// ── call_name(SiteHash, CalleeName) ──────────────────────────────────────────
call_name(e, n) :- call_decl("client", _, n, _, e, _).

// ── call_dotted(SiteHash, CalleeDottedPath) ──────────────────────────────────
// The full written path. THE ONLY DURABLE CROSS-RUN KEY for a callee, and therefore the
// input to name-based library linking: no hash minted by the client parse can match one
// minted by the library parse.
call_dotted(e, d) :- call_decl("client", _, _, d, e, _), d != "".

// ── call_callee_expr(SiteHash, CalleeExprHash) ───────────────────────────────
// The node that DENOTES the callee.
// (a) NO RECEIVER — the CALL's CALLEE child: a NAME_REFERENCE (`combine_all(...)`) or a
//     CLS_REFERENCE (`cls(name)`).
// (a) A CALLEE CHILD, whatever the receiver shape. Keyed on the ROLE and not on
//     receiverKind, because those two disagree for a subscript call: `TABLE["double"](21)`
//     carries receiverKind=SUBSCRIPT (its receiverText is `TABLE`) while still putting the
//     SUBSCRIPT node under edgeRole=CALLEE. Switching on receiverKind sent that site to
//     the RECEIVER branch, found no RECEIVER child, and produced no callee at all — the
//     08-callables golden caught it the moment the parser started emitting SUBSCRIPT_CALL
//     instead of DYNAMIC_CALL. The role is the reliable signal.
call_callee_expr(site, callee) :-
    expr_parent("client", site, "CALLEE", _, callee).
// (b) WITH A RECEIVER — the CALL's RECEIVER child, which is the WHOLE
//     ATTRIBUTE_ACCESS (`self.render`, `Base.make_default`, `super().describe`). Note
//     this is the schema's deliberate choice of role name so Java's
//     `java_expression(_, "RECEIVER", ...)` pattern ports unchanged; the actual receiver
//     OBJECT is one level further down, under ATTRIBUTE_OBJECT.
// (b) otherwise the RECEIVER child, which for a call IS the whole ATTRIBUTE_ACCESS
//     (`self.render`, `Base.make_default`, `super().describe`) — the schema keeps that
//     role name so Java's pattern ports unchanged. The actual receiver OBJECT is one level
//     further down, under ATTRIBUTE_OBJECT.
call_callee_expr(site, callee) :-
    !expr_parent("client", site, "CALLEE", _, _),
    expr_parent("client", site, "RECEIVER", _, callee).

// ── call_callee_is_value(SiteKey) ────────────────────────────────────────────
// The site's callee is a VALUE EXPRESSION — it has a CALLEE child. True for a bare name
// (`f(x)`), a CLS_REFERENCE (`cls(x)`), and a SUBSCRIPT (`TABLE["k"](x)`); false for an
// attribute call, whose callee arrives under RECEIVER instead.
//
// THIS, NOT receiverKind, IS THE RIGHT GATE. `TABLE["double"](21)` reports
// receiverKind=SUBSCRIPT (its receiverText is `TABLE`) while its callee sits under
// CALLEE, so every value-callee rule keyed on receiverKind="NONE" skipped it — the
// registry entry resolved and the call site still had no target. The 08-callables golden
// caught it the moment the parser began classifying that site SUBSCRIPT_CALL rather than
// DYNAMIC_CALL.
call_callee_is_value(site) :- expr_parent("client", site, "CALLEE", _, _).


// ── call_receiver_object(SiteHash, ObjectExprHash) ───────────────────────────
// THE RECEIVER OBJECT: `self` in `self.render(x)`, `Base` in `Base.make_default()`,
// `super()` in `super().describe()`, `self.base` in `self.base.describe()`,
// `Sibling()` in `Sibling().describe()`. This is the expression whose TYPE decides
// dispatch, and getting it from the attribute node rather than from
// receiverExpressionLinkHash keeps the rule independent of which node the parser chose
// to point that FK at.
call_receiver_object(site, obj) :-
    call_callee_expr(site, callee),
    expr_parent("client", callee, "ATTRIBUTE_OBJECT", _, obj).

// ── call_arg(SiteHash, Position, ArgExprHash) ────────────────────────────────
// Positional arguments, anchored on the parent being a CALL (see the header).
call_arg(site, pos, a) :-
    call_expr(site, e), expr_node("client", "CALL", _, _, e),
    expr_parent("client", e, "ARGUMENT", pos, a).

// ── call_kwarg(SiteHash, KeywordName, ArgExprHash) ───────────────────────────
// 12,000 keyword arguments corpus-wide. Without argumentKeywordName these could only be
// linked positionally, and would be silently lost.
call_kwarg(site, k, a) :-
    call_expr(site, e), expr_node("client", "CALL", _, _, e),
    expr_parent("client", e, "KEYWORD_ARGUMENT", _, a),
    expr_keyword_arg("client", k, a).

// ── call_star_arg / call_dstar_arg(SiteHash, ArgExprHash) ────────────────────
// `f(*args)` / `f(**kwargs)`. Their presence is exactly what makes argFlowIsPrecise
// false, so they are projected as sites of PROVABLE imprecision.
call_star_arg(site, a) :-
    call_expr(site, e), expr_node("client", "CALL", _, _, e),
    expr_parent("client", e, "STAR_ARGUMENT", _, a).
call_dstar_arg(site, a) :-
    call_expr(site, e), expr_node("client", "CALL", _, _, e),
    expr_parent("client", e, "DOUBLE_STAR_ARGUMENT", _, a).

// ── call_from(SiteHash, CallerMethodHash) ────────────────────────────────────
// The enclosing method. NEVER "" — a module-level call is owned by the synthetic
// <module> MODULE_INITIALIZER and a class-body call by <classbody>, so unlike Java
// there is no type fallback to write here. The conservation guard would surface it
// immediately if that ever changed.
call_from(site, caller) :- csite(site, s), call_context("client", _, caller, _, _, s), caller != "".

// ── call_enclosing_type(SiteHash, TypeHash) ──────────────────────────────────
// The class the call is written inside; "" outside a class. THIS IS THE super() SLICE
// POINT — the schema says so explicitly (col 9 gives the slice point, type_base.position
// gives the order).
call_enclosing_type(site, t) :- csite(site, s), call_context("client", _, _, t, _, s), t != "".

// ── call_module(SiteHash, ModuleHash) ────────────────────────────────────────
call_module(site, m) :- csite(site, s), call_context("client", _, _, _, m, s).

// ── call_scope(SiteHash, ScopeHash) ──────────────────────────────────────────
call_scope(site, sc) :- csite(site, s), call_context("client", sc, _, _, _, s).

// ── call_is_dynamic(SiteHash) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
// callKind=DYNAMIC_CALL: getattr / setattr / eval / exec. UNRESOLVABLE BY
// CONSTRUCTION, and the schema marks it so the engine can report exactly that rather
// than silently emitting no edge (982 getattr/setattr sites, 51 eval/exec corpus-wide).
call_is_dynamic(site) :- call_decl("client", "DYNAMIC_CALL", _, _, site, _).
